  "slug": "rice-adventitious-2660",
  "summary": "Wheat and rice root anatomy and stress-linked regulators are now resolved by axis position, allometry, spatial expression and dosage effects.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantitative control of anatomy in grasses and its functional consequences. In wheat, nodal root anatomy differs by culm position beyond root area scaling, and allometric correction exposes distinct genetic associations. Metaxylem number is remodelled along root axes in wheat and other temperate grasses, with spatial transcriptomics and hydraulic modelling linking vessel patterning to water transport. In rice, OsWOX11 promotes thick AR2 roots and a promoter variant (Hap3) shows higher activity, while an RLC3-KNOX-lignin module governs leaf rolling and drought tolerance. In creeping bentgrass, miR164-NAC dosage separates growth, drought and salt responses via AsNHX1. New here is finer resolution: positional, allometric, spatial and dosage-sensitive analyses. The Arabidopsis NAC38-NAC58-FUS3 seed coat study is a related example of transcriptional control of tissue differentiation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
